From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: android-kitkat-tls12 <android-kitkat-tls12@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 23/32] Port of 9ca6641, 978e2e1: SSL_MODE bits and
 SSL_OP_TLSEXT_PADDING of OpenSSL 1.0.1u

This series moved external/openssl to OpenSSL 1.0.1u
(external_openssl/0001-0003). Its ssl.h numbers the SSL_MODE bits as
1.0.1j does: SSL_MODE_SEND_FALLBACK_SCSV is 0x80 (upstream), and the
Android handshake_cutthrough patch was moved to 0x200, the value of
Android 5.1, because 1.0.1u uses 0x20 for SSL_MODE_SEND_CLIENTHELLO_TIME.
NativeCrypto still had the KitKat 1.0.1e values (cut-through 0x20,
SCSV 0x200, from 6c9609af5f): the TLS_FALLBACK_SCSV pseudo-suite turned
on cut-through instead of sending the SCSV, and the NPN test looked for
the wrong bit.

This takes the constants of conscrypt 9ca6641 ("Track upgrade to
OpenSSL 1.0.1j", Android 5.1): SCSV 0x80, cut-through 0x200.
SSL_MODE_CBC_RECORD_SPLITTING (0x100) is not added: our OpenSSL does not
have the 5.1 patch that defines it.

It also takes conscrypt 978e2e1 ("Assert that the padding extension is
enabled by default"): SSL_OP_TLSEXT_PADDING (0x10) and its assert in
NativeCryptoTest.test_SSL_new. In 1.0.1u, SSL_OP_ALL (set by
SSL_CTX_new in the JNI) includes SSL_OP_TLSEXT_PADDING, so a ClientHello
of 256-511 bytes, which old F5 load balancers stall on, is padded to 512
bytes (RFC 7685), as in Android 5.0+. The new test
SSLSocketTest.test_SSLSocket_ClientHello_padding checks it on the wire:
a short ClientHello has no padding extension, and the same ClientHello
with a long SNI host name (about 384 bytes unpadded) carries it and is
at least 512 bytes long.

Every other SSL_OP_*/SSL_VERIFY_* constant NativeCrypto mirrors has the
same value in the 1.0.1u headers.

Android 4.2 port (android-4.2.2_r1) of patch 0023 of the 4.4 series,
made from its Android 4.3 port. Paths and names: libcore/crypto of 4.4
(org.conscrypt, org_conscrypt_NativeCrypto.cpp in libjavacrypto) is
libcore/luni on 4.2 (org.apache.harmony.xnet.provider.jsse,
org_apache_harmony_xnet_provider_jsse_NativeCrypto.cpp in libjavacore).
The Java values are those of 4.4 (SCSV 0x80, cut-through 0x200), which
the OpenSSL 1.0.1u headers of the 4.2 tree
(patches/jb/4.2/external_openssl) define. The value replaced differs:
stock 4.2 Java used cut-through 0x40 (its OpenSSL 1.0.1c value; 0x40 is
SSL_MODE_SEND_SERVERHELLO_TIME in 1.0.1u), and SCSV 0x200 came from
patch 0001. The comment names the 4.2 value. The new NativeCryptoTest
assertion keeps the 4.2 int s2.
